A minimal parent-child pair showing both directions at once — props flowing down, an event flowing up:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState("");

  return (
    <SearchBox
      value={query}                    // data down
      onChange={(next) => setQuery(next)}  // event up
    />
  );
}

function SearchBox({ value, onChange }) {
  return (
    <input
      value={value}
      onChange={(e) => onChange(e.target.value)}
    />
  );
}
```

`SearchBox` never calls `setQuery` and never touches `Parent`'s state directly — it only knows `value` (a prop) and `onChange` (a function it was handed). `Parent` remains the single source of truth for `query`, and `SearchBox` stays reusable: swap in any parent that supplies a `value` and an `onChange`, and it works identically. Vue and Angular express the same shape with `v-model`/`emit` and `@Input`/`@Output` respectively — the wiring differs, the ownership rule doesn't.

```jsx
function TodoItem({ todo }) {
  function markDone() {
    todo.done = true;   // mutating the prop object directly
    forceRender();       // "but I re-rendered it!"
  }
  // ...
}
```

This often *appears* to work on the first click, because the object reference didn't change and some rendering paths read it anyway — then breaks unpredictably: the parent still holds `done: false` in its own state, so the next time the parent re-renders for any other reason, it overwrites the child's mutation and the checkbox flips back. React's strict mode and Vue's reactivity system both specifically warn about this class of bug because it's so common.

The fix is always the same: **the child asks, the parent updates.** Call a callback (`onToggle(todo.id)`), let the parent update its own state immutably, and let the new `done` value flow back down as a prop. Never write to a prop's fields, even if the language lets you.
